1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,719
How can a single city ban overturn thousands of votes?

2
00:00:04,719 --> 00:00:10,880
We often believe that those who block environmental reforms do so out of sheer climate denial.

3
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:15,480
Take Milan's Area B, a rule that suddenly banned circulation

4
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:17,480
for thousands of cars.

5
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:22,600
While many residents felt little impact, owners of the banned vehicles faced

6
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:26,120
an unexpected cost of €3,750.

7
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:28,719
Yet there is a surprising finding.

8
00:00:28,839 --> 00:00:33,280
These penalized drivers kept the same views on protecting the environment

9
00:00:33,280 --> 00:00:36,399
as the rest of the population.

10
00:00:36,399 --> 00:00:39,200
It is the so-called pocketbook vote.

11
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:42,840
Voters do not punish the administration for being too green,

12
00:00:42,840 --> 00:00:48,200
but because they find it deeply unfair that the transition weighs only on some citizens.

13
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:50,719
That frustration turned into politics.

14
00:00:50,719 --> 00:00:57,480
Those affected raised their vote for the League, then in opposition, by 13.5%.

15
00:00:57,480 --> 00:01:01,480
The vote against Area B does not express anti-environmentalist positions,

16
00:01:01,480 --> 00:01:03,920
but pure economic resentment.

17
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:08,600
If the green transition creates inequalities, the voter presents the bill.
